================================================================================
  Why any of this
================================================================================

The Sun is loud. Just beneath its surface, convection carries heat outward in
cells that rise, overturn, and collide, and all that churning makes noise. The
noise gets trapped. Sound heading down is bent back up by the rising temperature
of the deep interior, at a depth called the lower turning point, and sound
heading up is reflected back down where the gas thins out at the surface.
Between those two boundaries is an acoustic cavity, waves that fit it reinforce
themselves, and the Sun rings.

The trapped waves are p-modes, p for pressure, because pressure is the restoring
force pushing the gas back each time it overshoots. Nearly everything in this
pipeline is one. There is a second family riding along, the f-modes, which sit
at n = 0 and are surface waves rather than sound waves, restored by gravity
instead of pressure.

Nobody strikes this bell on purpose. The convection is just noisy, and some of
that noise happens to land at frequencies the cavity likes, so modes are
continually being kicked and continually dying away. That is stochastic
excitation, and it has a consequence worth holding onto: the same turbulence
that starts a mode ringing is what stops it.

Millions of these modes ring at once, and each one has spent its entire life
inside the Sun. That is the whole point. You cannot see the solar interior, but
you can hear it, because a wave's frequency depends on the sound speed
everywhere along the path it travelled. Learning the inside of the Sun this way
is global helioseismology, and it is what this group does.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  How a mode gets its name
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Three integers.

  n   Radial order. How many times the wave changes sign between the reflecting
      layers below and above it. Roughly, how many nodes it has going down.

  l   Degree. How finely the pattern is divided across the solar surface. Low l
      is a slow global swell; high l is a fine ripple. l also sets the depth:
      high-l waves turn around shallowly and never reach the core, low-l waves
      plunge all the way through.

  m   Azimuthal order. Which of the 2l+1 orientations of that surface pattern
      you happen to be looking at. On a perfectly round, non-rotating star, all
      2l+1 would ring at exactly the same frequency. That sameness is a
      degeneracy, and it is fragile.

The Sun is not that star. It rotates, so a mode travelling with the rotation and
one travelling against it come back to you at different frequencies. The
degeneracy is lifted and the 2l+1 spread out into a band, which is rotational
splitting. It is inconvenient in Part 2, and it is also how the Sun's internal
rotation gets measured in the first place.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  What we actually measure
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Take the Doppler velocity of the solar surface and project it onto the pattern
belonging to one (l, m). Doing that for every (l, m) is spherical harmonic
decomposition, and it turns one image of the Sun into a set of time series, one
per mode pattern. Fourier transform a time series, square it, and you have a
power spectrum with a peak wherever a mode lives. Every peak carries three
numbers, and they answer three different questions.

  nu      Where the peak sits. Set by the sound speed along the wave's path.
          This is the number that structure inversions have been built on.

  Gamma   How wide the peak is, measured as the full width at half maximum. The
          linewidth. A mode that damps quickly cannot hold a sharp frequency, so
          the linewidth is the damping rate wearing different units, and since
          what damps these modes is the convection that excited them, Gamma
          reads out the convective layers directly.

  A       How tall the peak is. Set by the balance between how hard convection
          kicks the mode and how fast the mode bleeds energy away.

nu has been mined for decades. Gamma has not, and that is the one this group is
going after. If you read one more thing after this page, read bd why fit.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  The six parts, end to end
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

  1  download    Pull the (l, m) power spectra from JSOC at Stanford. One file
                 per degree, l = 0 through 1000, for every 3-day date range.

  2  mavg        Collapse the 2l+1 spectra at each degree into one, then
                 renormalize onto a common scale. Out: 1001 spectra per date
                 range.                                        (bd why mavg)

  3  fit         Fit the peaks in those spectra and read off nu, Gamma, and A
                 for every mode that can be resolved.          (bd why fit)

  4  convsub     Convert what Part 3 produced, then difference it. Not against
                 one designated reference, but against every other date range in
                 the run: all 24 taken two at a time. That is 276 pairs, and it
                 is exactly where the 276 binl and 276 binnu files come from.
                 Each difference is binned by degree and by frequency, in each
                 of the 13 variable directories.

  5  activity    Assemble the solar activity indices covering the same dates,
                 and difference those the same pairwise way.

  6  regression  Regress the binned parameter differences against the activity
                 differences, transpose, and locate the zero crossings, meaning
                 the frequencies at which a mode parameter stops responding to
                 activity one way and starts responding the other.

Pairing everything with everything is worth a moment. Differencing against a
single quiet reference would give 23 contrasts and would make the whole result
lean on that one range being what you claimed it was. Pairing gives 276, and
nothing has to be designated.

A run is 24 consecutive 3-day date ranges. Three days is short on purpose: the
question is how these numbers move as the Sun's activity moves, and a long
average would smear the motion away. The cost is paid twice. A finite record of
length T has a frequency resolution no finer than 1/T, so three days buys you
bins about 3.9 microhertz wide and nothing sharper. And three days of any single
(l, m) time series is mostly noise with a suggestion of a peak in it. Part 2 is
what makes the second problem survivable.

